    "summary": "Oluluyia comprises a continuum of Western Kenyan Bantu varieties documented by Hobley (1902), Purvis (1909), Roscoe (1915), Johnston (1902, 1919), Stam (1929), and modern Logoori & Lubukusu studies (Payne, Pacchiarotti & Bosire 2016).",
    "content": "As documented in C. W. Hobley (*Eastern Uganda*, 1902, pp. 43–51, 61–88, 92–95), Rev. J. B. Purvis (*Through Uganda to Mount Elgon*, 1909, pp. 265–357), Rev. John Roscoe (*The Northern Bantu*, 1915, pp. 161–196), Sir Harry H. Johnston (*Comparative Study of the Bantu Languages*, 1919, Vol. I, pp. 7, 19, 73–85, 757 & Vol. II, pp. 22–24, 225), Rev. N. Stam (*The Bahanga*, 1929), and Doris L. Payne, Sara Pacchiarotti & Mokaya Bosire, eds. (*Diversity in African languages*, Language Science Press, 2016, Chs. 9, 11, 12, 15, 25 & 28):\n1. **Historical Varieties Recorded in 1902–1929**:\n   - **Lu-Wanga / Luhanga (Stam 1929, Hobley 1902, Johnston 1902 *Lurimi* & 1919 *5a. Lu-wanga*)**: Spoken in the Wanga Kingdom (Buhanga) ruled by Nabongo Mumia; uses *okhu-* infinitives and *ekhyi-/evi-* (Class 7/8).\n   - **Lu-Nyara (Johnston 1902 & 1919 *5. Lu-nyara*)** & **Lu-Kabarasi (Johnston 1902)**: Spoken in Kakamega/Busia/Lake Victoria north shore.\n   - **Ketosh / Lu-Konde / Lu-Masaba / Lubukusu (Hobley 1902, Purvis 1909, Roscoe 1915, Johnston 1902/1919 *6–6a* & Safir & Selvanathan 2016 Ch. 25)**: The Bukusu–Masaba cluster, characterized by double preprefixes (*umu-ndu*, *ba-ba-ndu*, *kumu-bano*, *khu-* infinitive) and transitive reciprocal constructions with `-an` + `ba-b-eene khu b-eene`.\n   - **Lulogooli / Luragooli (Maragoli, `[rag]` JE41, BFBS 1921 & Payne et al. 2016 Chs. 9, 11, 12, 15)**: Southern dialect contrasting *v* where central Luhya has *kh* (*makuva* vs. *amakhuwa* 'words').\n2. **Cross-Dialect Lexical Correspondences**:\n   - **1st Person Singular (\"I / me\")**: *esie* (Wanga), *eshie* (Kisa), *ise* (Nyole/Bukusu/Masaba), *inze* (Logoli).\n   - **King / Chief**: *omwami (mwami)* / *nabongo* (Stam 1929 & Hobley 1902).\n   - **God / Supreme Creator**: *Were* / *Nasayi* (Purvis 1909, p. 296 & Stam 1929, pp. 154, 159–160; Nasayi = 'the one adored' < sayo 'to adore').",
